; charge_cost_overflows_the_budget_word: an allocation whose byte cost does
; not fit the budget's own integer width is refused, not admitted.
;
; The allocation budget is a u64 in the Rust VM and an unbounded integer in
; xqvm_py. A size of 9223372036854775807 costs 8 bytes per variable, which
; is about 7.4e19 and does not fit a u64 at all. Rust used to compute that
; product with saturating arithmetic and accumulate it with a saturating
; add, so the charge read as u64::MAX -- and at the budget set here, also
; u64::MAX, a total of u64::MAX is not *greater than* the limit and the
; charge was admitted. The budget stopped binding exactly where the request
; was largest, and the run went on to fault on the size instead, while
; xqvm_py's exact arithmetic refused at the charge.
;
; A cost that does not fit a u64 exceeds every u64 budget by definition, so
; MemoryLimitExceeded is forced rather than chosen. This vector pins it at
; the only budget that can reach the condition.
;
; Reaching the charge at all needs a budget no host would set. That is the
; point: the vector exists so the top of the range is specified rather than
; left to whichever arithmetic an implementation happens to use.

PUSH 9223372036854775807
BQMX r0
HALT
